<span class="kicker">2026-08-14 / RIDGEWELL GUIDES</span><h1>How to Build a Raised Paver Walkway</h1>
</div>
</section>
<section class="section article-section"><div class="wrap editorial-layout"><article class="prose blog-content"><p>A raised walkway sits a few inches above the surrounding grade rather than flush with it, which changes a few things about how it’s built compared to a standard, flush paver walkway. The extra height needs something to hold it in place along both edges, and that raised profile happens to solve a specific problem a lot of homeowners deal with, a low, wet, or boggy section of yard where a flush walkway would just sit in the same water problem it’s trying to walk over.</p>
<p>At Ridgewell Landscape and Design, we build raised walkways across the Denver area, including Broomfield, Parker, and the surrounding communities, both as a design choice and as a practical solution for yards with drainage challenges. This guide covers how to build a slightly raised walkway, why it works well for wet or low-lying areas, and how to construct the edge restraints and retaining elements that keep a raised profile in place over time.</p>
<h2><b>What Makes a Raised Walkway Different From a Flush One</b></h2>
<table><tbody><tr><td><b>Factor</b></td><td><b>Flush Walkway</b></td><td><b>Raised Walkway</b></td></tr><tr><td>Height relative to grade</td><td>Level with surrounding ground</td><td>A few inches above surrounding ground</td></tr><tr><td>Edge containment</td><td>Standard edge restraints</td><td>Sturdier edge restraints or short retaining elements</td></tr><tr><td>Drainage benefit</td><td>Follows surrounding grade</td><td>Elevates walking surface above wet or low areas</td></tr><tr><td>Base requirements</td><td>Standard depth</td><td>Similar depth, plus attention to edge stability</td></tr><tr><td>Visual effect</td><td>Blends into the yard</td><td>Reads as a more defined, intentional feature</td></tr></tbody></table><p>The core construction, excavation, base, sand, laying pavers, is largely the same regardless of whether a walkway sits flush or raised. What changes is how the edges are handled, since a raised profile has more exposed side area that needs to be held in place rather than blending directly into surrounding soil.</p>
<h2><b>Why Raise a Walkway: Common Reasons</b></h2>
<h3><b>Solving a Wet or Low-Lying Area</b></h3>
<p>A section of yard that stays damp, holds standing water after rain, or simply sits lower than the surrounding grade is a poor candidate for a flush walkway, since the walkway would essentially sit in the same drainage problem. Raising the walkway a few inches above that low point keeps the walking surface dry and usable even when the ground around it is temporarily wet.</p>
<h3><b>Creating Visual Definition</b></h3>
<p>Beyond solving a practical problem, a raised walkway simply reads as more intentional and defined within a landscape, standing slightly apart from the surrounding lawn or garden bed rather than blending flush into it. This is a design choice some homeowners make even without a drainage issue driving the decision.</p>
<h3><b>Separating a Walkway From Loose Groundcover</b></h3>
<p>In yards with mulch, gravel, or loose groundcover bordering a walkway, a raised profile helps keep that material from migrating onto the walking surface the way a flush walkway more easily allows.</p>
<h2><b>Assessing Whether Your Site Needs a Raised Approach</b></h2>
<p>Before committing to a raised design, it’s worth understanding why a particular section of yard tends to be wet or low in the first place. Sometimes it’s simply the natural low point of an otherwise well-draining yard, and a raised walkway is a straightforward, effective solution. Other times, the wetness points to a bigger drainage issue, poor grading elsewhere in the yard, a downspout emptying directly into that area, or generally poor soil drainage, that a raised walkway alone won’t fully resolve. If standing water in the area seems unusually persistent or severe, it’s worth addressing the underlying drainage cause alongside, or before, raising the walkway itself.</p>
<h2><b>Edge Containment: What Holds a Raised Walkway in Place</b></h2>
<h3><b>Sturdier Edge Restraints for Modest Rises</b></h3>
<p>For a walkway raised just an inch or two above grade, reinforced edge restraints, anchored more frequently than on a flush walkway, are often sufficient to hold the edge in place. The added height isn’t significant enough to require a full retaining structure, but it does put more lateral pressure on the edge than a flush installation, so extra anchoring points help compensate.</p>
<h3><b>Short Retaining Elements for a More Pronounced Rise</b></h3>
<p>For a walkway raised several inches, a short retaining element, a single course of retaining wall block, a paver-matched curb, or a treated timber edge, gives a more solid, permanent containment than edge restraints alone can reliably provide. This becomes increasingly important as rise height increases, since the soil and base material behind a taller edge exert more outward pressure over time.</p>
<h3><b>Choosing a Retaining Material That Matches the Walkway</b></h3>
<p>Whatever retaining element you choose, matching its material and color to the walkway pavers, or intentionally choosing a complementary contrast, keeps the raised edge looking like a designed feature rather than a mismatched afterthought. Our guide on<a href="/services/retaining-walls/"> retaining walls</a> covers material options that work well as both structural and visual elements alongside hardscape.</p>
<h2><b>What You’ll Need</b></h2>
<p><b>Materials</b></p>
<ul><li>Pavers, plus 5 to 10 percent extra for cuts and waste</li><li>Crushed gravel or road base, 4 to 6 inches deep depending on soil type</li><li>Retaining block, timber, or paver-matched curb material for the edges</li><li>Landscape fabric</li><li>Coarse bedding sand</li><li>Polymeric sand for joints</li><li>Reinforced edge restraints and spikes, or retaining adhesive if using block</li></ul>
<p><b>Tools</b></p>
<ul><li>Shovel and spade</li><li>Rubber mallet</li><li>String line and stakes</li><li>4 foot level</li><li>Paver saw or angle grinder with a masonry blade</li><li>Push broom</li></ul>

