<h2><b>Step by Step: Building a Raised Paver Walkway</b></h2>
<h3><b>Step 1: Assess the Site and Confirm Drainage Needs</b></h3>
<p>Walk the planned path and note the low or wet areas driving the need for a raised design, and consider whether any underlying drainage issue should be addressed alongside the walkway itself.</p>
<h3><b>Step 2: Mark the Layout</b></h3>
<p>Mark the walkway’s path and width using stakes and string, keeping in mind that a raised walkway’s visible footprint includes the retaining edge, not just the walking surface itself.</p>
<h3><b>Step 3: Excavate for the Base</b></h3>
<p>Excavate to a depth accounting for base, sand, and paver thickness, similar to a standard walkway, generally 7 to 9 inches total, even though the finished surface will end up raised above the surrounding grade.</p>
<h3><b>Step 4: Install the Edge Retaining Structure First</b></h3>
<p>For a raised walkway, install the retaining edge elements, whether that’s block, timber, or a paver curb, before building the main base, since the retaining structure needs its own compacted footing and defines the boundary the rest of the base will fill within.</p>
<h3><b>Step 5: Build and Compact the Gravel Base</b></h3>
<p>Add crushed gravel in 2 inch layers within the retained area, compacting thoroughly with a plate compactor after each layer, same as with a standard flush walkway.</p>
<h3><b>Step 6: Add Landscape Fabric and Bedding Sand</b></h3>
<p>Roll landscape fabric over the compacted base, then add and screed about 1 inch of coarse sand to create a level surface for the pavers.</p>
<h3><b>Step 7: Lay the Pavers</b></h3>
<p>Place pavers following your chosen pattern, working within the retained edges. Our guide on<a href="/blog/how-to-lay-a-paver-walkway/"> how to lay a paver walkway</a> covers pattern and technique fundamentals that apply here as well.</p>
<h3><b>Step 8: Cut Border Pavers to Fit the Retained Edge</b></h3>
<p>Measure and cut border pieces to fit snugly against the retaining structure, rather than leaving a gap between the pavers and the edge material.</p>
<h3><b>Step 9: Secure Additional Edge Restraints if Needed</b></h3>
<p>Depending on your retaining approach, additional standard edge restraints may still be needed at the paver level itself, anchored against the retaining structure for extra stability.</p>
<h3><b>Step 10: Fill Joints and Final Compact</b></h3>
<p>Sweep polymeric sand into the joints, mist to activate, and run the plate compactor over the finished surface one final time.</p>
<h2><b>Managing Grade Transition at Either End</b></h2>
<p>A raised walkway needs a thoughtful transition where it meets surrounding flush ground at each end. This is typically handled with a short, gentle ramp of soil and plantings blending the raised edge back down to grade, or in some cases a single low step if the rise is more pronounced. Leaving an abrupt, unaddressed edge where the raised section suddenly meets flush ground creates both a visual rough spot and a potential trip hazard.</p>
<h2><b>Common Mistakes on Raised Walkway Projects</b></h2>
<ul><li>Using standard edge restraints alone on a walkway raised more than an inch or two, without adequate reinforcement</li><li>Not addressing an underlying drainage issue that’s causing the wet area in the first place</li><li>Leaving an abrupt, unfinished transition where the raised section meets surrounding grade</li><li>Choosing a retaining material that clashes visually with the walkway pavers</li><li>Skipping a proper footing for the retaining structure, leading to it shifting under lateral base pressure over time</li></ul>
<h2><b>Why Raised Walkways Are Worth Getting Professional Input On</b></h2>
<p>A modest rise with reinforced edge restraints is a reasonable DIY undertaking for a careful homeowner. Once the rise becomes more significant, or the underlying reason for raising the walkway involves a genuine drainage problem beyond just a naturally low spot, professional input helps ensure the retaining structure is built correctly and that the root drainage issue is actually being addressed rather than just walked over.</p>
<p>With over 15 years of building raised walkways and matching retaining solutions across the Denver area, our team at Ridgewell Landscape and Design can help assess whether your wet or low-lying area needs a raised walkway, broader drainage work, or some combination of both. Explore our<a href="/services/paver-patios/"> paver patio services</a> to see examples of raised and retained hardscape features.</p>

<h2><b>Frequently Asked Questions</b></h2>
<h3><b>How high should a raised paver walkway be?</b></h3>
<p>Most raised walkways sit anywhere from an inch to a few inches above surrounding grade, enough to keep the walking surface above a wet or low area without requiring a full retaining wall structure.</p>
<h3><b>Do I need a retaining wall for a raised walkway?</b></h3>
<p>Not always. A modest rise of an inch or two can often be held with reinforced edge restraints, while a more pronounced rise generally benefits from a short retaining element like block, timber, or a paver-matched curb.</p>
<h3><b>Will a raised walkway fix a drainage problem in my yard?</b></h3>
<p>It can solve the immediate issue of a walking surface sitting in standing water, but if the wetness stems from a broader drainage issue, that underlying cause is worth addressing as well for a complete fix.</p>
<h3><b>How do I transition from a raised walkway back to ground level?</b></h3>
<p>A gentle sloped transition of soil and plantings, or a single low step for a more pronounced rise, is the typical approach to avoid an abrupt edge where the walkway meets surrounding grade.</p>
<h3><b>Does a raised walkway need a different base than a flush one?</b></h3>
<p>Base depth requirements are generally similar, but the retaining edge structure needs its own proper footing, which is an additional construction step a flush walkway doesn’t require.</p>

<h2><b>Ready to Build a Raised Walkway for Your Yard</b></h2>
<p>A raised walkway solves a real practical problem for wet or low-lying areas while also giving your landscape a more defined, intentional feature.</p>
<p>Ridgewell Landscape and Design has been building raised walkways and retaining solutions across Denver, Broomfield, Parker, and the surrounding communities for over 15 years. If you have a low or wet area you’re trying to solve for,<a href="/contact/"> reach out for a free quote and consultation</a>.</p>

